  "notes": "The research's defining sentence is a negation: g-funk harmony \"usually without the use of samples.\" Everything is synthesized and played, which shows up here as the highest `quantizeStrength` and the lowest `velocityVar` of any Bay entry — a drum-machine kit, drier and more synthetic than the LA records — sitting under four genuinely played synth layers. Those four layers are why this is not mobb minimalism: bass, pad, lead and counter-line all get real density, so the melody runs 4–7 a bar and the countermelody keeps the parent's 0.55 ratio where the sparser Vallejo entries cut it to a third. The harmony is his documented `i–VI–VII` / `i–iv` with minor-7th pads, which is sevenths at half the extension mass. The bass is the one place he is conservative: synth bass sliding between roots, so `followRootsProb` 0.78 with `glideProb` 0.82 — high glide, low invention. Loop-static forms and long instrumental intros come straight from the research's note that he presents finished beats for freestyling. ⚠ `modes` are authored because the parent's Death Row moods pin 90–100 and his band is 84–94. ⚠ `medium` per the research's medium-high — the no-samples method is documented, the note densities are inferred from the comparison it draws (busier than mobb minimalism, less dense than Above the Law).",
